First, we will create a context for the tooltip.

```jsx
import React, { createContext, useState } from 'react';

export const TooltipContext = createContext();

export const TooltipProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [tooltip, setTooltip] = useState(null);

  const closeTooltip = () => {
    setTooltip(null);
  };

  const openTooltip = (content) => {
    setTooltip({ content, close: closeTooltip });
  };

  return (
    <TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip }}>
      {children}
      {tooltip && <div className="tooltip">{tooltip.content}</div>}
    </TooltipContext.Provider>
  );
};
```

Now, we can use this context in our Tooltip component to open and close the tooltip.

```jsx
import React, { useContext } from 'react';
import { TooltipContext } from './TooltipContext';

const Tooltip = ({ children }) => {
  const { openTooltip, closeTooltip } = useContext(TooltipContext);

  const handleMouseEnter = (event) => {
    const tooltipContent = event.currentTarget.getAttribute('data-tooltip');
    if (tooltipContent) {
      openTooltip(tooltipContent);
    }
  };

  const handleMouseLeave = () => {
    closeTooltip();
  };

  return (
    <div
      onMouseEnter={handleMouseEnter}
      onMouseLeave={handleMouseLeave}
      data-tooltip={children}
    >
      {children}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Tooltip;
```

Finally, we can use the Tooltip component in our NextJS app.

```jsx
import Tooltip from './Tooltip';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <TooltipProvider>
      <div>
        <Tooltip>Hover over me to see the tooltip</Tooltip>
        {/* More components here */}
      </div>
    </TooltipProvider>
  );
};

export default App;
```

Now, when you hover over the Tooltip component, it will open the tooltip with the provided content. If you hover over another Tooltip component, it will close the previously opened tooltip and open the new one.

