Here's a solution for your problem using TypedArray and Bitwise operations:

```javascript
function packEntities(entities) {
  const buffer = new Uint16Array(Math.ceil(entities.length / 2));

  let offset = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < entities.length; i++) {
    const entity = entities[i];
    const id = entity.id & 0xF; // Masking to get the 4 least significant bits
    const size = (entity.id >> 4) & 0x7; // Shifting and masking to get the 3 middle bits

    buffer[offset++] = (id << 13) | (size << 9); // Packing id and size into a 16-bit value
  }

  return buffer.buffer;
}

function unpackEntities(buffer, length) {
  const entities = [];
  let offset = 0;

  while (offset < buffer.byteLength && offset < length) {
    const value = new Uint16Array(buffer, offset, 1)[0];
    const id = (value >> 13) & 0xF; // Shifting and masking to get the 4 least significant bits
    const size = (value >> 9) & 0x7; // Shifting and masking to get the 3 middle bits

    entities.push({ id, size });
    offset += 2; // Move to the next 16-bit value
  }

  return entities;
}
```

In the `packEntities` function, we create a Uint16Array buffer and pack the entities into it using bitwise operations. The id and size are packed into a 16-bit value by shifting and bitwise ORing them together.

In the `unpackEntities` function, we extract the entities from the buffer by shifting and masking the 16-bit values to get the id and size. We continue this process until we reach the desired length or the end of the buffer.

